  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a volt?",
      "a": "Voltage is the push that drives current, like water pressure in a pipe. A 1.5 volt battery gives a gentle push; a 230 volt socket a much stronger one.",
      "asks": [
        "What does voltage mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an amp?",
      "a": "An amp measures current, how much electric charge flows past a point each second, like litres of water per second in a pipe. A phone charger might give 2 amps; a kettle draws about 13.",
      "asks": [
        "What does current mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is resistance in the water picture?",
      "a": "Resistance is like a narrow section of pipe that makes it harder for water to flow. For the same push, more resistance means less current.",
      "asks": [
        "What is resistance like?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a watt?",
      "a": "A watt measures power, how fast energy is used. Power equals volts times amps, so 230 volts times 13 amps is about 3000 watts, a typical kettle.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you work out watts?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is a battery like a water pump?",
      "a": "Yes. A pump lifts water so it can flow down again; a battery lifts electric charge to a higher energy, its voltage, so it can flow round the circuit and do work.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the battery do in the water picture?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Power in watts equals voltage in volts multiplied by current in amps.",
    "The volt is named after Alessandro Volta and the watt after James Watt."
  ],
